The full analysis is in `/home/chrisdavidson/Projects/agent-router/integrations/first-principles/runs/structured-summary-live/personal-general-2/.first-principles/analysis-20261001T021809Z.md`. This message is only a summary; please read the file in full, since it has every calculation and its recomputation.

**Disclosed:** The self-check loop ran a second time. On the first pass the ground-truths section scored weak, because the four figures I looked up online only supported medium-confidence conclusions. I fixed it by adding one more reasoning chain, C6, built only from those looked-up figures and rated high confidence. The second pass cleared.

**Recommendation: put the $60,000 toward the mortgage principal (Option A).** Confidence is medium. Before paying:
- confirm there is no prepayment penalty;
- confirm the servicer will apply the money to principal, not to future payments;
- open a home-equity line of credit (HELOC) but don't draw on it, so the money isn't completely locked in the house;
- check the next statement afterwards.

**Why A wins:**
- **What A is worth.** With the monthly payment kept at $1,535.24, the extra payment leaves the loan balance $111,913.09 lower at year 10. That is a guaranteed 6.25% a year after tax (6.432% effective annual rate), because the mortgage interest isn't deducted.
- **What B has to earn to tie.** The index fund needs a gross return of 6.62% a year if you never sell, or 7.52% if you sell at year 10 and pay the 18% gains tax.
- **What B is likely to earn.** Starting from today's 1.06% dividend yield, a rough build-up of the fund's return gives a middle estimate of 6.56%, with a range of 2.81% to 9.31%. In the middle case B ends at $103,286 after tax, $8,627 short of A.
- **Safe alternatives.** Even a 10-year Treasury paying 5.29% with no tax at all earns less than 6.25%. So A is the best riskless use of the money.
- **B's downside.** The stock market lost 9.14% in total over 2000–2009, so B can end the decade below $60,000.

**Why confidence is medium, not high:** The mortgage and tax figures come from your description, not from documents I could check. The future market return is a forecast that no source can confirm.

**What would change the answer:**
- you start itemising deductions;
- you refinance at a much lower rate (refinancing at 5% in year 3 drops A to about $102,579);
- you are comfortable with risk *and* expect returns near the 1928–2025 average of 10.02% a year;
- you need more cash than the six-month reserve covers.

Separately, the Gmail, Google Calendar and Google Drive connectors need authorising in your claude.ai connector settings before they can be used. This analysis didn't need them.

